## Final Validation Results

The replication engine was applied to the full type 1, 3, 4 and 9 portfolio (n = 266,112 facilities) and compared against the original SRS values `NB_MATURITE_IRBA` and `NB_DURATION_IRBA`.

### Overall reproduction quality

The duration — the regulatory quantity used in the IRB capital formula — is reproduced within ±0.1 years on **96.7%** of the portfolio, with an RMSE of 26.4 days. The raw maturity is reproduced less tightly (90.1% within ±0.1 years) because the regulatory clip [1, 5] absorbs part of the deviation when computing the duration.

### Performance by amortization type

Types 4 (bullet) and 9 (other) are reproduced exactly through the rule `NB = R`, with residual error of less than 1 day. Type 1 (linear) reaches 99% match within ±0.1 years through the closed-form formula `(n+1)/24`. Type 3 (progressive) carries the bulk of the residual gap (95.8% match), concentrated on Crédits Revolving and CAV & autres facilities, which follow an internal SRS logic that cannot be inferred from the variables available in `srs_X_capret`.

### Error distribution

85% of facilities have a duration error within ±10 days, which is calendar-rounding noise. The remaining 15% are split between a +10 to +30 day band (~11%, attributable to month-discretization effects) and a tail beyond ±60 days (~4%, attributable to the Revolvers/CAV residual perimeter).

### Summary

The replication engine reproduces SRS duration values to within 0.1 years on 96.7% of the IRBA credit risk portfolio. Residual deviations are documented and traceable to either calendar-day rounding (the dominant component) or the Revolvers/CAV product family, for which a deterministic rule could not be identified from the source data and which is documented as a known scope limitation.
